.TH CJSON_SERIALIZE 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
cjson_serialize \- serialize a DOM tree to a compact JSON string
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cjson.h>

char *cjson_serialize(cjson node);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Walks the DOM tree under node and makes a compact JSON string with
no whitespace.  A NULL node gives the string "null".

The serializer writes the members of each object in insertion order,
which cjson_dictionary_first(3) describes, and the elements of each
array in their index order, so the output for one tree is the same on
every call and in every process.  A document that parses and
serializes again keeps the order of its members.

The serializer writes a double as the shortest decimal form that
reads back as the same value, and that form holds up to 17 significant
digits.  It always uses '.' as the decimal separator, as RFC 8259
asks, whatever the LC_NUMERIC locale of the calling thread.  It
writes an integer with %lld, and a double that is not finite (Inf and
NaN) as "null".

The output is always valid JSON text.  Every string that a tree
holds, whether a value or an object key, is well-formed UTF-8; see
cjson_parse(3) and cjson_create_string(3) for how the library checks
this wherever bytes enter a tree.  The serializer therefore writes
string content unchanged and adds only the escapes that JSON itself
needs.

The serializer refuses a subtree that nests more than 500 levels
deep and returns NULL for it.  Each array or object is one level, in
any combination, and a scalar adds none.  The limit is the same as
the limit of the parser; only the construction API, through
cjson_list_push() and cjson_dictionary_set(), can build a deeper
tree, because that API has no depth bound while a parsed document
does.  This is a policy limit, not a guess about the stack size of
the calling thread: the serializer walks the nesting with an explicit
worklist on the heap, so a tree at the limit costs the same small,
fixed amount of native stack as a flat one.  An ordinary tree never
comes near this depth.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
A null-terminated string on the heap on success.
NULL when the allocation fails, or when node nests more than 500
levels deep.
The string comes from the allocator of the root node.  The caller
frees it with cjson_serialize_free() when the default allocator
created the tree, and with cjson_serialize_free_mp() and the same
allocator when a custom allocator created the tree.  Calling
cjson_serialize_free() on a buffer from a custom allocator gives that
buffer to plain free(), which never allocated it.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cjson_serialize_pretty (3),
.BR cjson_serialize_free (3),
.BR cjson_serialize_free_mp (3),
.BR cjson_create_string (3),
.BR cjson_dictionary_first (3),
.BR cjson_parse (3)
